WEBVTT

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:03.763
Almost every language on Earth
says tea in one of two ways.

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:05.219
Some say chai.

00:00:05.434 --> 00:00:06.364
Some say tea.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.530
The reason is how the tea travelled.

00:00:08.811 --> 00:00:11.201
It began in China, where the word was cha.

00:00:11.452 --> 00:00:14.062
Overland, tea went west
along the Silk Road.

00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:15.547
And the word went with it.

00:00:15.803 --> 00:00:17.353
In Persia, it became chay.

00:00:17.564 --> 00:00:18.819
In Turkey, çay.

00:00:18.854 --> 00:00:20.114
In Russia, chay.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.660
In India, it became chai.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.731
But tea also left China by sea.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.807
In the port of Fujian,
people did not say cha.

00:00:27.172 --> 00:00:28.377
They said te.

00:00:28.413 --> 00:00:31.983
Dutch traders loaded tea
there in the early 1600s.

00:00:32.069 --> 00:00:33.759
They carried the word te to Europe.

00:00:34.099 --> 00:00:37.509
So English got tea,
German got Tee, and French got thé.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.130
Portugal is the exception.

00:00:39.556 --> 00:00:42.606
Its traders bought from Macau,
and so they say chá.

00:00:42.826 --> 00:00:46.816
So if your word for tea starts with ch,
it travelled by land.

00:00:47.107 --> 00:00:49.457
If it starts with t, it came by sea.

